WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.319
Scenario: A senior executive emails finance from a plausible

2
00:00:03.319 --> 00:00:06.638
account and asks for an urgent confidential transfer to

3
00:00:06.638 --> 00:00:09.956
a new beneficiary. The message says normal approval can

4
00:00:09.956 --> 00:00:13.275
be bypassed because the deal is sensitive. Safest response:

5
00:00:13.275 --> 00:00:16.594
Option C: Hold the transfer and verify through a

6
00:00:16.594 --> 00:00:19.912
pre-established independent channel and mandatory dual-authorization process. High-impact payment

7
00:00:19.912 --> 00:00:23.969
instructions require independent corroboration and policy-based approval, regardless of apparent seniority.

8
00:00:23.969 --> 00:00:27.288
Option A is unsafe because Authority and secrecy are

9
00:00:27.288 --> 00:00:30.606
not substitutes for identity verification or delegated financial authorization.

10
00:00:30.606 --> 00:00:33.925
Option B is unsafe because A smaller unverified transfer

11
00:00:33.925 --> 00:00:37.981
is still unauthorized and may confirm that the account is active.

12
00:00:37.981 --> 00:00:41.300
Option D is unsafe because A compromised mailbox can

13
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.356
be controlled by the attacker, including replies in the same thread.

14
00:00:45.356 --> 00:00:48.675
In practice: Pause payment, use contact details already held

15
00:00:48.675 --> 00:00:51.994
in trusted records, validate the beneficiary independently and retain

16
00:00:51.994 --> 00:00:55.313
the approval trail. Key takeaway: For high-impact financial changes,

17
00:00:55.313 --> 00:00:58.631
verify identity and authorization outside the channel carrying the

18
00:00:58.631 --> 00:00:59.000
request.
